0..78 Comment "// Two migrations to one world, a value held at the seat and read there again."
79..81 Comment "//"
82..159 Comment "// It witnesses a capture crossing in: the second migration\'s body reads what"
160..190 Comment "// the first held at the seat."
191..193 Comment "//"
194..270 Comment "// The first migration holds a value at the seat and brings its handle back."
271..350 Comment "// `let n at seat` opens the handle, binding what it holds at the seat, and the"
351..430 Comment "// second migration reads it there. In one process a handle is its content, and"
431..486 Comment "// each crossing, in or back, is a commit then a deref."
487..489 Comment "//"
490..565 Comment "// - see: Murphy, Crary and Harper, \"Type-Safe Distributed Programming with"
566..591 Comment "//   ML5\", TGC 2007, § 3"
592..656 Comment "// - see: Rainey et al., \"LoCalMem\", PACMPL 10 (ICFP 2026), § 5"
657..720 Comment "// - see: `open.gandr` — what crosses back from one migration"
722..788 Comment "// The operator\'s and the seat\'s worlds, each declaration\'s entry."
